category: science

entry electron-cloud
term: Electron Cloud
related_terms: electron
related_terms: atom
related_terms: nucleus
quick_facts: It replaces the old picture of electrons as tiny planets
quick_facts: Darker parts show where an electron is more likely to be
quick_facts: The cloud is about 100,000 times wider than the nucleus
quick_facts: Each shape of cloud is called an orbital

sources:
    {"organization":"Britannica","title":"Electron","url":"https://www.britannica.com/science/electron"}

definition:
    A picture of where the electrons of an atom are found. Instead of racing around the nucleus on neat rings, the electrons are somewhere in a fuzzy region around it. The cloud is darker where an electron is more likely to be. The cloud is huge compared with the nucleus, so most of an atom is empty space.

debunking_snippet:
    “Electrons circle the nucleus like planets.” They have no fixed path. The cloud shows where they are likely to be.
